1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,580
 All right, onto the demo.

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:05,160
 So here's a proof agent skill file.

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:09,540
 It contains the procedure we want the verifier to follow.

4
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:13,040
 We give it two things, the original request and the diff.

5
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:16,420
 So what did we ask for and what actually changed?

6
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:18,940
 We leave out the workers self-assessment

7
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:21,380
 so the verifier can inspect the work

8
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:24,360
 without inheriting the workers conclusion.

9
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:26,700
 Now let's put that procedure to work.

10
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:29,260
 This is team X-rays GitHub Actions workflow.

11
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:32,340
 It takes the skill and runs it against a pull request.

12
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:34,180
 There is one setting I want you to notice,

13
00:00:34,180 --> 00:00:36,180
 block on fail, false.

14
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:37,180
 That's deliberate.

15
00:00:37,180 --> 00:00:40,020
 In this particular repo, the verifier posts findings,

16
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:42,180
 but it's better it doesn't block a merge.

17
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:44,420
 CI, CoQL, and the dependency audit

18
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:46,820
 still enforce the merge requirements.

19
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:48,660
 We can inspect the agent's reasoning,

20
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:51,820
 challenges findings, and decide what needs fixing,

21
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:53,500
 but we haven't given its judgment control

22
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:54,920
 of the merge bottom.

23
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:58,240
 So what happens when we give it a real change?

24
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:02,740
 This is a PR 75, fixed release team aware reports.

25
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:05,380
 It changes report rendering and release automation.

26
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:08,940
 That gives the verifier a concrete claim to check.

27
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:11,180
 Does the code support that description?

28
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:15,480
 And what happens along the paths that this changes touches?

29
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:17,500
 We're looking for evidence that we can follow back

30
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:21,560
 into the diff, a file, a line, the code behind the finding.

31
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:23,580
 And so what did it catch?

32
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:27,100
 The first verdict is failed with two findings marked high.

33
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:30,220
 The verifier flags a release tag interpolated

34
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:32,020
 directly into a shell command,

35
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:35,340
 creating a command injection risk.

36
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:39,660
 It also flags unskipped data going into the report's HTML,

37
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:42,580
 creating the XSS risk in the web view.

38
00:01:42,580 --> 00:01:45,100
 Both findings point to a specific code

39
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:48,820
 that gives us something concrete to go investigate and fix.

40
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:50,820
 So did the fix actually fix it?

41
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:52,900
 Well, here's the correction commit.

42
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:56,180
 Harder and report rendering and the release upload.

43
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:58,640
 But adding a fix doesn't sell the question.

44
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:02,180
 We run the verifier again against the updated diff.

45
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:05,040
 Now it needs to show where the correction addresses

46
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:07,580
 the findings of fix and a commit message

47
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:09,760
 still needs the evidence behind it.

48
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:13,680
 So at this time, we still have some issues open.

49
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:14,980
 The verdict is partial.

50
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:18,180
 Reports, the verifier reports the injection issue fix,

51
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:20,340
 but leaves questions about avatar handling

52
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:22,680
 and the CI configuration.

53
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:25,100
 That distinction matters when you're reading the results

54
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:26,660
 because something you couldn't confirm

55
00:02:26,660 --> 00:02:30,380
 is not automatically something that is broken.

56
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:32,820
 We need to know which concern was resolved

57
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:35,380
 and what still needs investigation.

58
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:36,940
 So what is pass cover?

59
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:40,100
 Here we get a pass for the controls inspected on this diff.

60
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:41,180
 That's scope.

61
00:02:41,180 --> 00:02:43,580
 It doesn't certify the whole application as secure.

62
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:48,080
 So we can follow up on the result from the actions run.

63
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:50,700
 That verify took 42 seconds and finished green.

64
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:53,260
 So that tells us that the job actually completed.

65
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:56,140
 We still read the verdict to understand what I found.

66
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:57,860
 But from that PR comment,

67
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:00,100
 another developer can come and open this run

68
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:01,420
 and inspect what happened.

69
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:03,540
 They don't have to take my explanation

70
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:06,580
 or the agents handlined on good faith.

71
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:09,540
 And that is what makes this useful in a team setting

72
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:12,180
 because someone else can actually follow the evidence

73
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:13,780
 and challenge the conclusion.

74
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:16,360
 And if something stays unresolved

75
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:19,100
 like this firing over it here, which is partial,

76
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:21,500
 then there's a workflow permission question

77
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,500
 where the verifier couldn't resolve from the diff to long.

78
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:27,060
 So we leave it open rather than turning missing context

79
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:28,540
 into a confident answer.

80
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:30,300
 That's where I wanna end this demo.

81
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:33,220
 We've seen findings, corrections and conclusions

82
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:35,060
 with a defined scope.

83
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:38,100
 I don't know, needs a place in your verification workflow.

84
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:40,780
 And here it tells us exactly where human follow up

85
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:41,780
 is still needed.

